From 729b86c59a4cd91484a65c7c835c4cbf919ed64b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Connor Baker <ConnorBaker01@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 19:42:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] mm: memcontrol: let memcg OOM scans see exiting OOM victims

Since a72f73c4dd9b ("cgroup: Don't expose dead tasks in cgroup"),
css_task_iter_advance() skips a task with PF_EXITING once its
signal->live is 0, unless the caller passes CSS_TASK_ITER_WITH_DEAD,
which 60f21a264930 ("cgroup, sched_ext: Include exiting tasks in cgroup
iter") added for sched_ext. mem_cgroup_scan_tasks() passes only
CSS_TASK_ITER_PROCS, so a memcg OOM scan stops returning an OOM victim
once its last thread has dropped signal->live in do_exit(). That is
before exit_mm(), and so before exit_mmap() sets MMF_OOM_SKIP on the
victim's mm.

oom_evaluate_task() depends on seeing the victim until then: it aborts
the scan on an OOM victim whose mm is not yet MMF_OOM_SKIP. With the
victim hidden, a charge that reaches the memcg OOM killer while the
victim is still freeing its memory selects and kills another task.

Pass CSS_TASK_ITER_WITH_DEAD. css_task_iter_advance() then filters as
it did before a72f73c4dd9b: CSS_TASK_ITER_PROCS still skips threads
that are not group leaders, and leaders on dying_tasks without live
threads. A leader whose whole group has exited is never put on
dying_tasks (do_cgroup_task_dead() adds a leader there only while
signal->live is non-zero), so it is returned only until its final
context switch unlinks it, as before a72f73c4dd9b. The callers already
handle exiting tasks: oom_badness() and dump_task() skip a task none of
whose threads has an mm, oom_kill_process() lets an exiting victim go
through task_will_free_mem(), and __oom_kill_process(), for
memory.oom.group, skips a victim with no mm.

Seen in NixOS VM tests on 7.2.9, where a memcg OOM-killed a shell's
child that buffered random bytes (zswap's rejects held in swap cache):
a second task was killed in 3 of 10 runs, and in 6 of 29 runs with a dd
loop in the same memcg. 6.18.55, which has neither the deferred cgroup
unlink nor a72f73c4dd9b, killed once in all 10 and all 39 runs. With
bpftrace on out_of_memory(), oom_evaluate_task(), __oom_kill_process()
and the victim's do_exit() and exit_mmap(), 7.2.9 showed 11 scans that
passed the victim's place in the task list after its do_exit(), 7 of
them during its exit_mmap(). None evaluated the victim; each selected
another task, which was killed in 9 and already exiting in 2. On
6.18.55 all 17 such scans evaluated the victim, with PF_EXITING set and
signal->live 0, and aborted.

Fixes: a72f73c4dd9b ("cgroup: Don't expose dead tasks in cgroup")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5 bpftrace
---
 mm/memcontrol.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/mm/memcontrol.c b/mm/memcontrol.c
index 7138ebc6ef..36d6f8b737 100644
--- a/mm/memcontrol.c
+++ b/mm/memcontrol.c
@@ -1371,7 +1371,13 @@ void mem_cgroup_scan_tasks(struct mem_cgroup *memcg,
 		struct css_task_iter it;
 		struct task_struct *task;
 
-		css_task_iter_start(&iter->css, CSS_TASK_ITER_PROCS, &it);
+		/*
+		 * Include exiting tasks: oom_evaluate_task() must see an OOM
+		 * victim until its mm is MMF_OOM_SKIP, or another task is
+		 * killed while the victim's memory is still being freed.
+		 */
+		css_task_iter_start(&iter->css, CSS_TASK_ITER_PROCS |
+				    CSS_TASK_ITER_WITH_DEAD, &it);
 		while (!ret && (task = css_task_iter_next(&it))) {
 			ret = fn(task, arg);
 			/* Avoid potential softlockup warning */
-- 
